{
  "answer": "Bell's theorem proved that you cannot have both locality and definite pre-existing values for properties like spin or polarisation. You may keep either, but not both. Locality means that what happens here cannot be instantly affected by what happens far away; definite pre-existing values mean that the property has a definite value before it is measured, rather than being created by the act of measurement.\n\nWhat it does not prove is anything about minds or consciousness. It does not prove that reality is non-local in the sense that our thoughts can directly affect distant events. It does not prove the existence of a non-physical realm or any form of psychic phenomenon. It is a theorem about the behaviour of physical systems, and its implications are for our understanding of physics, not for our understanding of the human experience.\n\nIn plain words, Bell's theorem says that if you want to explain the behaviour of particles in certain experiments, you have to give up either the idea that what happens here can't be instantly affected by what happens far away, or the idea that those particles had definite properties before they were measured. That's all it says. The rest is interpretation and speculation.",
